# Local environment defaults for courtyard. During installation, copy this file:
#
#     cp .env.default .env
#
# then uncomment and change what your machine needs. `.env` is gitignored; both
# docker compose and the Makefile read it. Everything works without a .env when
# the defaults suit your machine. Write values bare, KEY=value, no quotes: make
# passes the line as it is, and a quoted value reaches docker and the hub with
# its quote characters.

# Host port for the compose postgres: 26432, deliberately not 5432, so the hub never
# collides with or touches a postgres of your own. The hub, tests and runbook scripts
# all follow this variable; a full DATABASE_URL (if you ever set one) wins over it.
#COURTYARD_PG_PORT=26432

# One machine, one courtyard database: every checkout and install shares the compose
# project `courtyard` (its volume, its containers). For a second, isolated instance
# give it its own project name, postgres port and hub port:
#COURTYARD_COMPOSE_PROJECT=courtyard

# Port for the hub and its WebUI.
#COURTYARD_PORT=2626

# Host port for Adminer, the postgres browser started by `make db-ui`.
#COURTYARD_ADMINER_PORT=8080

# Hub log verbosity on stdout: INFO (default), WARNING or ERROR (DEBUG also works).
# Request lines log at their real severity: a 4xx response as WARNING, a 5xx as
# ERROR. WARNING therefore keeps failures visible while routine 200 lines go quiet.
#COURTYARD_LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# Hub memory, similarity search. An OpenAI-compatible embeddings endpoint on this
# machine turns courtyard_recall from full-text into hybrid (full text + vectors);
# without it recall is full-text only. Ollama serves one after `ollama pull nomic-embed-text`:
#COURTYARD_EMBEDDINGS_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1/embeddings
#COURTYARD_EMBEDDINGS_MODEL=nomic-embed-text
# A non-local endpoint sends message bodies off this machine; it needs the explicit
# override (and, if the service wants one, a key):
#COURTYARD_EMBEDDINGS_ALLOW_REMOTE=1
#COURTYARD_EMBEDDINGS_API_KEY=
